  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nAn upper level low is currently over the northeastern US with upper \ntroughing over much of the eastern CONUS. Additional upper troughing \nextends from the northwestern US through the Central Great Basin \ninto the Four Corners area. Upper ridging continues between these \ntwo features over the Northern Plains and Upper Mississippi Valley. \nAt the surface, expansive high pressure stretches from Ontario down \nthrough the Mississippi Valley. Rain showers are ongoing across \nsouthwestern Kansas and down into the Texas Panhandle due to 850 mb \nWAA. As we move into tonight, an upper shortwave will move into \nnorthern Texas with rain chances shifting east into Sunday morning. \nThis will bring rain chances early Sunday morning for areas west of \nI-135 and in the southwestern portion of our area, though the best \nchances will likely remain in northern Oklahoma. By late Sunday \nmorning, chances will shift to near the KS/OK border with low \nchances possible through Sunday afternoon as the upper low continues \nto slide across Texas. By Sunday night, drier conditions are \nexpected across our area with activity remaining south in Oklahoma. \n\nOn Monday, upper ridging will build back into the Plains with a \ngradual warming trend expected throughout the week. An upper trough \nwill move onshore to California on Tuesday and then make its \nway to the Central Rockies on Wednesday. This will increase \nsurface troughing over the Central Plains on Tuesday and \nWednesday bringing gusty southerly winds to the area. The \nstrongest winds are likely in central and portions of south-\ncentral KS. Sustained winds are forecast up to 30 mph with gusts\nup to 40-45 mph. Low rain chances will be possible over eastern\nKansas Wednesday night as the upper trough slides across the \nPlains though there is still some uncertainty. By Thursday, this\nshortwave is progged to have moved to the Great Lakes area. \nAdditional rain chances will be possible into next weekend as a \ndeeper upper trough moves onshore and progresses east. Stay \ntuned.",
